.TH "UBERTOOTH\-DUMP" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-dump\fR \- output a continuous stream of received bits
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-dump [\-b] [\-c | \-l] [\-d <filename\.bin>]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
This tool dumps the raw bitstream received from the CC2400 radio to STDOUT or a file (if specified with \fB\-d\fR)\. It can configure the radio to demodulate Classic Bluetooth with \fB\-c\fR (default), or Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) with \fB\-l\fR\. Optionally it can decode the binary data and print ASCII 0 and 1 using \fB\-b\fR\.
.P
The capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-b\fR
Print ASCII 0 and 1 instead of raw data
.TP
\fB\-c\fR
Classic Bluetooth modulation (default)
.TP
\fB\-l\fR
Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) modulation
.TP
\fB\-d <filename\.bin>\fR
Dump to file instead of stdout
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-dump\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside it\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-d\fR names its file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Without \fB\-d\fR, standard output carries the binary symbol stream and nothing else\. Errors, warnings and the per\-block timestamp go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: /no/such/dir/capture\.bin: No such file or directory\fR\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-d' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after the dump is interrupted (Ctrl\-C)\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take, for a \fB\-d\fR file that cannot be opened, and when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened\.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ubertooth\-util(1): sets the receive squelch (\fB\-z\fR); SQUELCH describes how long it lasts
.P
ubertooth(7): overview of Project Ubertooth; OPTION VALUES describes how option values are written
